 "implications": {
  "jipoe": "Analyst assessment derived from cited sources; validate before use. STEP 1: The cyberspace OE includes cloud identity tenants (Entra ID/M365), SaaS providers and IT service providers. For this actor, key terrain is identity infrastructure, not network perimeters. STEP 2: Residential-proxy sourcing defeats IP reputation and geolocation, so detection terrain is authentication telemetry. STEP 3: The template is low-and-slow password spray, then a foothold in an overlooked or legacy account without MFA, then OAuth app abuse or privilege grants, then mailbox and document collection. A parallel option is a supply-chain or provider compromise. STEP 4: MLCOA is persistent collection on NATO and US government decision-making and on IT providers. MDCOA is a supply-chain compromise of a DoD or DIB software or cloud provider giving broad access during a crisis. Candidate indicators: new OAuth app consents or role grants (full_access_as_app), spray patterns against legacy accounts, and RDP files arriving by email [S1][S2].",
  "jcc2": "Analyst assessment derived from cited sources; validate before use. (1) Identity is the primary sensor: JCC2 situational awareness must include cloud sign-in and app-consent logs from DoD and partner tenants. (2) Third-party risk: a breach at a provider (cloud, IT service, software vendor) is a DoD event, so reporting agreements with providers are part of C2. (3) MFA and legacy-account hygiene across test and non-production tenants is a mission assurance requirement. (4) Counterintelligence fusion: SVR collection is foreign intelligence, so coordinate cyber findings with CI channels."
